        "submission": {
          "verdict": "malicious",
          "reasoning": "On sysmonlinux-tcontreras-attack-range-643, the root user ran the script ./mod_sudoer.sh from /home/ubuntu (sh, proc-cde358715c). The script started two sudo commands back to back. The first was 'sudo echo evil_user ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: ALL' (proc-8f783cab26), which spawned the trigger /bin/echo (proc-5113b0fdb3). The second was 'sudo echo root ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: ALL' (proc-3956796274). Writing passwordless sudo rules for a non-standard account called evil_user from an ad-hoc script matches T1548.003 sudoers abuse, which gives persistent privilege escalation. sudo's only network traffic was a DNS lookup to the local resolver 127.0.0.53, so there is no external C2. I did not confirm that a sudoers file was actually written, and I did not check who launched the script (proc-d345bba067). Response: isolate the host, review /etc/sudoers and /etc/sudoers.d and remove any NOPASSWD entries for evil_user or root, delete mod_sudoer.sh, and investigate or disable the evil_user account.",
